T22.01|Portfolio objectives specify what the money must achieve, while constraints define acceptable choices. Return needs should be considered with liquidity, horizon, tax, legal requirements and loss capacity. A desired high return does not create the capacity to bear the risk needed to pursue it.~~Start from liabilities and goals rather than a product list. Different portions of wealth can have different horizons and access requirements.|Return objective: Required or desired outcome.;;Risk objective: Acceptable uncertainty and loss.;;Constraints: Liquidity, horizon, tax and other limitations.|An investor has ₹100 lakh and must pay ₹30 in one year, with the remainder supporting a twenty-year goal.~~Separate the near-term ₹30 need.;;Assess suitable access and risk for that liability.;;Evaluate longer-horizon capital under its own goals and constraints.~~One asset allocation need not fit both time horizons.~~No specific product or return is assumed in this example.|Define goals and cash dates.;;Assess loss capacity separately from preference.;;Document constraints before selecting assets.
T22.02|An investment policy statement records objectives, constraints, allocation rules, implementation responsibilities and review procedures. It reduces ad hoc changes driven by markets or emotion and gives managers a clear mandate.~~The policy should allow justified updates when circumstances change. It is not a promise that the chosen assets will meet every goal or that a fixed allocation remains suitable forever.|Strategic policy: Long-term objectives and allocation.;;Implementation rules: Eligible assets, limits and responsibilities.;;Review rules: Rebalancing and changed-circumstance triggers.|A hypothetical policy targets 60% growth assets and 40% defensive assets, with a 5-point review band.~~If growth weight reaches 67%, it breaches the stated upper band.;;Review rebalancing using costs, taxes and cash flows.;;Change the policy only if objectives or assumptions warrant it.~~Written rules distinguish implementation from improvisation.~~The percentages illustrate governance, not a recommended allocation.|Record objectives and limits.;;Assign decisions and custody responsibilities.;;Specify review, exceptions and reporting.
T22.03|Risk tolerance is willingness to accept uncertainty; risk capacity is the ability to withstand loss without undermining essential goals. Horizon influences recovery opportunities and liability timing but does not make losses impossible. Behaviour and capacity can differ sharply.~~An investor excited by risk may still have fragile income or near-term obligations. Conversely, financially resilient investors may prefer low uncertainty; a suitable mandate considers both.|Tolerance: Psychological preference and behaviour.;;Capacity: Financial ability to bear adverse outcomes.;;Horizon: Time until funds serve their purpose.|An investor accepts volatile assets emotionally but needs ₹50 lakh next year and has only ₹55 total liquid wealth.~~A 20% portfolio loss would reduce wealth to ₹44.;;The payment goal would then be underfunded.;;Assess protecting the essential liability separately from willingness to speculate.~~High willingness does not establish high capacity.~~Actual planning depends on other income, obligations and resources.|Quantify essential commitments.;;Assess behaviour under realistic loss.;;Match risk by goal and horizon.
T22.04|Diversification combines exposures whose outcomes do not move perfectly together. Portfolio risk depends on weights, volatilities and co-movement rather than the number of securities alone. Many holdings in one sector or funding channel can remain concentrated.~~Correlation is estimated and can rise under stress. Diversification reduces some risks but cannot eliminate broad market losses, liquidity constraints or shared tail exposures.|Asset diversification: Different economic exposure classes.;;Within-class diversification: Different issuers or businesses.;;Risk-factor diversification: Different underlying drivers.|Two equal-weight assets each have 10% volatility and zero correlation under an illustrative model.~~Portfolio variance = 0.25 × 0.01 + 0.25 × 0.01 = 0.005.;;Volatility is about 7.07%.;;At correlation one, volatility rises to 10%.~~Co-movement determines the diversification benefit.~~Stress dependence and liquidity can differ from the historical estimate.|Map economic risk factors.;;Aggregate correlated exposures.;;Stress co-movement and liquidity.
T22.05|Modern portfolio theory evaluates combinations using expected return, variance and covariance. For specified inputs and constraints, optimisation identifies portfolios offering favourable risk-return trade-offs. Expected returns are estimates and can dominate the instability of the solution.~~Mean-variance analysis compresses outcomes into two measures. Tail risk, non-normality, liquidity, taxes and investor liabilities can require additional tools and constraints.|Expected portfolio return: Weighted asset expectations.;;Portfolio variance: Weights and covariance together.;;Constrained optimisation: Best modelled choices within limits.|Asset A expects 8% and B 4%; weights are 60% and 40%.~~Expected return = 0.6 × 8% + 0.4 × 4% = 6.4%.;;Calculate risk from covariance, not by assuming return weights imply safety.;;Test alternative estimates before adopting an optimum.~~The 6.4% expectation is a model input-based average, not a guarantee.~~Implementation costs and changing inputs can alter the preferred mix.|Estimate inputs consistently.;;Use realistic constraints.;;Assess estimation and tail-risk limitations.
T22.06|The efficient frontier contains portfolios with the highest modelled expected return for a given variance, or lowest variance for a given expected return. The minimum-variance portfolio has the least variance among the feasible set. Inefficient portfolios are dominated under the same assumptions.~~Efficiency is conditional on inputs and constraints, not an absolute label. A mean-variance-efficient portfolio may still violate an investor's liquidity or downside requirements.|Global minimum variance: Lowest modelled variance.;;Efficient portfolio: Undominated mean-variance trade-off.;;Dominated portfolio: Another feasible choice improves the specified dimensions.|Portfolio A expects 6% at 10% volatility; B expects 6% at 8%; assume both meet identical constraints.~~B offers equal expected return with less modelled volatility.;;A is dominated on these two dimensions.;;Review tails, liquidity and estimation uncertainty before choosing B.~~Frontier comparisons depend on what the model measures.~~Equal volatility does not imply equal full-distribution risk.|Compare feasible portfolios.;;State inputs and constraints.;;Include risks omitted by variance.
T22.07|A capital allocation line combines a risk-free position with a selected risky portfolio under the model. The capital market line uses the model's efficient market portfolio. Its slope measures excess expected return per unit of portfolio volatility.~~Borrowing and lending rates may differ, and unlimited risk-free borrowing is unrealistic. The security market line instead relates individual expected return to beta, so these lines should not be confused.|CAL: Cash and a selected risky portfolio.;;CML: Cash and the theoretical market portfolio.;;SML distinction: Expected return versus systematic beta.|Risk-free return is 3% and risky portfolio expectation 9% with 12% volatility. Invest half in each, assuming no leverage.~~Expected return = 6%.;;Volatility is 6% if the risk-free asset is truly nonvolatile in the model.;;CAL slope = (9% − 3%)/12% = 0.5.~~Changing risky weight changes both expected return and volatility linearly under the assumptions.~~Real cash, inflation and funding constraints need separate assessment.|Distinguish portfolio and security lines.;;State funding assumptions.;;Match the risk-free asset to currency and horizon.
T22.08|CAPM uses one market factor; multifactor models attribute returns to several systematic exposures; arbitrage pricing theory motivates factor-related pricing under its assumptions. Factors can represent economic variables or empirical return patterns.~~Model alpha is residual performance after the selected risk adjustment. An omitted relevant factor can make ordinary risk compensation appear to be skill. Different models can therefore produce different alpha estimates.|CAPM: Market-beta benchmark.;;Multifactor model: Several specified exposures.;;APT framework: No-arbitrage factor-pricing logic.|A portfolio returns 10%; the risk-free rate is 3% and market return 8%, with beta 1.2.~~CAPM benchmark = 3% + 1.2 × 5% = 9%.;;Simple model alpha is 1 percentage point.;;Test whether other factor exposures explain that difference.~~Alpha is model-dependent, not automatically manager skill.~~Sampling uncertainty and fees also matter.|Specify factors and data.;;Separate risk compensation from residual return.;;Test robustness and costs.
T22.09|Strategic allocation establishes a long-term mix aligned with goals; tactical allocation makes temporary deviations based on views or signals. The tactical decision changes risk and requires an explicit benchmark, horizon and exit rule.~~A short-term market view should not silently rewrite the long-term policy. Position sizing, transaction costs and uncertainty determine whether a plausible view has useful portfolio consequences.|Strategic mix: Durable policy allocation.;;Tactical deviation: Temporary active position.;;Dynamic policy adjustment: Change due to altered goals or constraints.|Policy equity weight is 50%. A tactical view raises it to 60% for six months.~~Active equity exposure is +10 percentage points.;;Estimate portfolio downside if the view fails.;;Record the review date and costs of reversing the position.~~Tactical exposure should be evaluated relative to the policy portfolio.~~The allocation figures are hypothetical, not investment advice.|Preserve the policy baseline.;;Define signal, size and exit.;;Assess costs and downside capacity.
T22.10|Passive management seeks a specified benchmark exposure; active management makes deliberate deviations to achieve objectives such as excess return or risk control. Passive funds still require implementation decisions, and active performance should be evaluated after fees and relevant risk.~~A manager's gross outperformance is not the investor's net result. Benchmark selection and survivorship can materially affect apparent success.|Passive replication: Track a defined index.;;Active selection: Choose holdings differing from benchmark.;;Active allocation: Change exposure weights relative to policy.|An active fund earns 9% gross, charges 1.5%, and faces a comparable benchmark returning 8%. Ignore fee timing for this approximation.~~Net return is about 7.5%.;;Net benchmark-relative result is about −0.5 percentage point.;;Assess risk, taxes and multi-period evidence as well.~~Gross skill claims can disappear after cost.~~A different risk mandate requires a different comparison.|Define the relevant benchmark.;;Measure net investor outcome.;;Assess risk and repeatability rather than one year.
T22.11|Tracking error measures variability of active returns relative to a benchmark. Active share measures holdings-weight differences under a defined formula. Tracking difference measures realised return gap over a period. These measures describe different dimensions.~~A portfolio can have high active share with low tracking error if different holdings have similar factor exposures. Low tracking error does not mean no absolute market risk.|Tracking error: Standard deviation of active returns.;;Active share: Holdings-based deviation.;;Tracking difference: Period return gap.|A fund trails its benchmark by exactly 0.5 percentage point in each observed period.~~Tracking difference is −0.5 point each period.;;The active-return variability is zero in this simplified series.;;The fund can have low tracking error while consistently lagging.~~A stable gap and a variable gap are different problems.~~Fees, sampling and index conventions can explain persistent differences.|Match benchmark and return periods.;;Separate level from variability.;;Inspect holdings and factor exposure.
T22.12|Portfolio construction turns ideas into weights subject to risk, liquidity, concentration and mandate limits. Position sizing should reflect loss capacity and correlation with other holdings, not conviction alone. Aggregate exposure can hide when several ideas share the same economic driver.~~A target weight must also be executable at acceptable cost. Liquidity and leverage can force reductions during stress rather than at the manager's preferred time.|Equal-weight construction: Same capital weight per holding.;;Risk-aware construction: Weights reflect volatility and dependence.;;Constraint-based construction: Limits on concentration, liquidity or claims.|A ₹100 lakh portfolio allocates ₹10 to a stock whose scenario loss is 40%.~~Position scenario loss is ₹4.;;That is 4% of total portfolio before other holdings' losses.;;Add correlated scenario losses rather than assuming each position is isolated.~~A small capital weight can carry a large loss contribution.~~The scenario is illustrative, not a maximum possible loss.|Translate weights to loss contributions.;;Aggregate common factors.;;Include liquidity, leverage and mandate limits.
T22.13|Rebalancing returns allocations toward policy after market moves or cash flows. Calendar rules use dates; threshold rules use deviation bands; cash-flow rebalancing redirects contributions or withdrawals. The choice balances policy risk, trading costs and taxes.~~Rebalancing is not guaranteed to outperform in every market path. It maintains a chosen risk profile and may reduce winners while adding to losers.|Calendar rule: Review at set dates.;;Threshold rule: Act after weight deviation.;;Cash-flow rule: Use incoming or outgoing funds.|Start with ₹60 growth assets and ₹40 defensive assets. Growth rises to ₹72 while defensive stays ₹40.~~Total is ₹112; growth weight is 64.29%.;;To restore 60/40, target growth value is ₹67.2.;;Transfer ₹4.8 under the simplified cost-free model.~~Weights drift even without a new investment decision.~~Fees, taxes and product constraints affect actual execution.|Define policy bands.;;Use cash flows where feasible.;;Assess costs and changed circumstances.
T22.14|A benchmark should represent the mandate's investable opportunity and risks. It supports attribution and accountability only when defined before results are observed and measured consistently. A broad index may be inappropriate for a specialised, constrained or liability-driven portfolio.~~Changing a benchmark after poor performance can conceal the decision's result. Custom benchmarks need transparent weights, rebalancing and source rules.|Market benchmark: Representative index exposure.;;Policy benchmark: Mix reflecting strategic allocation.;;Liability benchmark: Performance relative to obligations.|A portfolio has policy weights 60% equities and 40% bonds. Assume index returns 10% and 5%.~~Policy benchmark return is 0.6 × 10% + 0.4 × 5% = 8%.;;Compare the mandate against this stated mix, not only the 10% equity result.;;Use consistent rebalancing and fee conventions.~~Benchmark choice changes the interpretation of performance.~~Other constraints may require further adjustments.|Match mandate and opportunity set.;;Define methodology in advance.;;Use consistent return and cost bases.
T22.15|Sharpe relates excess return to total volatility; Sortino uses a specified downside-deviation measure; Treynor uses beta; information ratio uses benchmark-relative return and tracking error. They are not interchangeable rankings.~~Small denominators, negative excess returns and unstable samples can make ratios misleading. Match return periods, annualisation and risk definitions, and consider drawdowns and tail risk as well.|Sharpe: Excess return/total standard deviation.;;Sortino: Return above target/downside deviation.;;Treynor: Excess return/beta.;;Information ratio: Active return/tracking error.|Assume annual return 10%, risk-free rate 4% and volatility 12%.~~Excess return is 6%.;;Sharpe ratio = 6%/12% = 0.5.;;For an information ratio, replace both numerator and denominator with appropriate benchmark-relative measures.~~The ratio describes one defined risk-adjusted outcome.~~It does not establish suitability, predict future returns or summarise every risk.|Match horizons and annualisation.;;Use the risk measure fitting the question.;;Assess sample uncertainty and omitted downside risks.
T22.16|Alpha is return unexplained by a selected benchmark or risk model. Attribution decomposes performance into choices such as allocation, selection, currency or factors under a stated method. Both require a coherent baseline and reconcile differently depending on conventions.~~A positive residual can come from skill, luck, data error or omitted risk. Evaluate costs, persistence and out-of-sample evidence before assigning a causal explanation.|Allocation effect: Different category weights.;;Selection effect: Different holdings within categories.;;Model alpha: Residual after selected risk adjustments.|A portfolio returns 9% while its appropriate benchmark returns 8%.~~Simple active return is 1 percentage point.;;Use attribution to determine which decisions contributed.;;Risk-model alpha can differ if the portfolio took additional systematic exposure.~~Outperformance and alpha are related but distinct measures.~~A single year's residual offers weak evidence of repeatable skill.|Define the benchmark and model.;;Reconcile contributions to outcome.;;Assess fees, uncertainty and persistence.
T22.17|Drawdown is decline from a previous peak; maximum drawdown is the largest such observed loss over a defined period. Recovery requires a gain calculated from the lower value, so losses and recovery percentages are asymmetric.~~Downside protection can involve allocation, liquidity, hedges or contractual payoffs, each with costs and limitations. A stop order is not guaranteed insurance against a gap.|Peak-to-trough drawdown: Loss from prior high.;;Recovery gain: Gain needed to regain the peak.;;Protection mechanism: Defined mitigation with residual exposure.|Portfolio value falls from ₹100 to ₹70.~~Drawdown is 30%.;;Recovery gain needed = 100/70 − 1 ≈ 42.86%.;;Assess whether liabilities can still be met during the decline.~~A 30% gain does not reverse a 30% loss.~~Historical maximum drawdown is not a bound on future losses.|Measure using consistent returns.;;Assess liability and liquidity effects.;;Read protection costs and failure conditions.
T22.18|Liability-driven investing focuses on funding specified obligations; goals-based investing organises assets around defined personal or institutional outcomes. Return is evaluated relative to the goal, not only a market index. Different goals can support different risk budgets.~~Matching requires attention to dates, currency, inflation and uncertainty. An attractive aggregate return can coexist with failure to meet a particular cash deadline.|Liability matching: Align dated payments.;;Funding-ratio management: Assets relative to obligation value.;;Goal segmentation: Allocate resources by purpose and horizon.|A fund has ₹100 lakh assets and obligations valued at ₹120 under its model.~~Funding ratio = 100/120 = 83.33%.;;A 10% asset gain raises assets to ₹110.;;If liability value simultaneously rises to ₹132, the funding ratio remains 83.33%.~~Positive asset return need not improve funded status.~~Liability valuation assumptions and contribution policy matter.|Define liabilities and goals.;;Match currency, dates and inflation.;;Evaluate funded status alongside asset performance.
T22.19|Endowments, pensions and other institutions combine investment with governance, spending or benefit obligations. Their horizons may be long, but cash needs, regulation and sponsor conditions differ. Illiquid assets require a plan for commitments, distributions and stressed access.~~A long horizon does not remove near-term liquidity risk. Spending rules and capital calls can require asset sales when public markets decline.|Endowment: Supports an organisation's spending over time.;;Pension: Funds benefit obligations under plan terms.;;Other institution: Mandate-specific assets and constraints.|An endowment has ₹100 lakh assets, with ₹40 illiquid. It needs ₹8 annual spending plus a ₹12 capital call during a market stress.~~Immediate need is ₹20.;;Assess available liquid resources after realistic market losses and restrictions.;;Review spending and commitment policies before adding illiquid exposure.~~Long-term capital still needs short-term payment capacity.~~Governance and policy definitions are central to the decision.|Map spending and benefit needs.;;Stress capital calls and liquidity.;;Align governance, mandate and risk capacity.
